pancake is one library with a lot of surface: five transports, four language
bindings, two codecs, and a C ABI under all of them. Those pieces are not
equally proven, and saying so plainly is more useful than a single badge would
be.

Every pluggable piece gets a support tier. A tier is not a judgement about how
finished the code looks. It answers one question: what runs automatically to
prove this still works, and who wrote the thing it is proved against?

## The tiers

**Tier 1 — proved against somebody else's code, on every push.** At least one
check behind the row talks to an implementation this project does not control:
the upstream OCapN test suite (`zig build ocapn-conformance`), a live
[Spritely Goblins](https://spritely.institute/goblins/) peer
(`zig build goblins-interop`), the upstream Syrup encoder, or a real
[iroh](https://www.iroh.computer/) network. `zig build check-external` runs
the whole set. If pancake drifts from the specification, one of them notices
without anyone remembering to look.

**Tier 2 — proved on every push, against pancake's own code.** The checks run
automatically and catch a break before it ships, but every party to them is
pancake. They prove the pieces agree with each other, not that the whole agrees
with the specification.

**Tier 3 — a proof exists, but nothing runs it for you.** There is a real check
in the tree and you can run it yourself. Expect the surface to work; do not
expect a regression to be noticed quickly.

## Where things stand

This table is held identical to the one in
[docs/stability.md](https://codeberg.org/jimisaacs/pancake/src/branch/main/docs/stability.md)
by a build check, and that file carries the evidence behind every row.

| Piece | Tier |
| --- | --- |
| The protocol core (model, codec, session, engine) | 1 |
| Syrup codec | 1 |
| `tcp` transport | 1 |
| `iroh` transport | 1 |
| Cap'n Proto codec | 2 |
| `keyed-tcp` and `inproc` transports | 2 |
| The actor layer and the persistent map (its snapshot store) | 2 |
| The Rust, TypeScript (Deno), Python, and Racket bindings | 1 |
| The TypeScript binding on Bun | 2 |
| C ABI | 2 |
| The relay, in all four runtimes | 2 |
| `webrtc` transport | 3 |
| Browser (`wasm32`) | 2 |
| macOS | 3 |
| Windows | not implemented |
| Mutation fuzzing | 2 |

These tiers describe the checks configured in the mirror workflow. They are
current evidence only when that
[workflow](https://github.com/jimisaacs/pancake/actions/workflows/check.yml) is
green for the commit you are looking at.

A word on two rows: Deno, Python, Rust, and Racket outrank the C ABI they
sit on because each runs a live Goblins conversation. Bun runs the shared
TypeScript source against pancake but not against Goblins.

`zig build fuzz` runs coverage-guided mutation fuzzing on every push, over every
surface that takes bytes somebody else chose. That means both codecs, from the framer that finds a
frame's edges to the decoder that reads it. It means the whole inbound path,
from stream bytes to the events an application sees. It means the byte-fed host
at read boundaries the fuzzer picks, and blocking reads through the spill that
carries a frame across two of them. It means handoff parsing and locator
parsing. Anything it finds becomes a saved input that the ordinary test
suite replays from then on.

Most of it looks for more than crashes. A decoded message has to survive being
re-encoded and decoded again unchanged, which catches a codec that reads a frame
differently from how it writes one. A session has to keep every table inside the
limit it declares, checked after each step against caps deliberately set low
enough to cross, because a table growing past its bound is a partner exhausting
your memory without anything ever crashing.

Caveats: each target searches on its own, and a run's own counter has to
advance for it to count, so a target that stops working fails the sweep by
name rather than hiding behind the others. The per-push budget is a standing
sweep, not a deep search. And it has found nothing yet, which you can read
either way.

The [full page in the repository](https://codeberg.org/jimisaacs/pancake/src/branch/main/docs/stability.md)
names, for every row, the exact checks behind its tier and the work that would
raise it. A build check compares its list of what runs on every push against
the continuous-integration script itself, so a tier cannot quietly outlive the
proof it rests on.

## What a tier is not

It is not an API promise. No type, function, or wire detail here is frozen, and
no version carries a compatibility guarantee.

It is not a security claim. Unreviewed code is unreviewed code at every tier,
and [SECURITY.md](https://codeberg.org/jimisaacs/pancake/src/branch/main/SECURITY.md)
is where that conversation belongs.

If you are evaluating pancake and one of these tiers matters to you, say so on
the [issue tracker](https://codeberg.org/jimisaacs/pancake/issues). A surface
with a user gets its proof wired in first, and
[Project &amp; status]($link.page('project')) maps where the whole climb stands.
